markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Module Overview
This professional learning module is made up of **five lessons:**
<div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px; max-width: 900px; margin: auto;">
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 15px;">
        <div style="width: 20%; max-width: 120px; aspect-ratio: 1 / 1; background: #FFFFFF; border-radius: 20px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0;">
            <img src="https://images.code.org/d7a2764589820d4023ca876e49c892c1-image-1740592077414.png" alt="Lesson 1 Icon" style="width: 65%; height: 65%; object-fit: contain; border-radius: 10px;">
        </div>
        <div style="flex: 1; background: #FFFFFF; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; font-size: 14pt; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
            <h3>Lesson 1 - Introduction to Web Apps</h3>
            <div><strong>⭐️ You are here!⭐️</strong></div>
            <div>Here's the foundation you'll return to all unit: what makes something a web app, and how users actually interact with one. You will build a shared vocabulary for the structural components you will be teaching throughout the unit.<br><br><em>10 minutes</em></div>
        </div>
    </div>
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 15px;">
        <div style="width: 20%; max-width: 120px; aspect-ratio: 1 / 1; background: #FFFFFF; border: 1px solid #FFFFFF; border-radius: 20px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0;">
            <img src="https://images.code.org/3d11b3154389d2caa405683ffda44ff6-image-1740592165845.png" alt="Lesson 2 Icon" style="width: 65%; height: 65%; object-fit: contain; border-radius: 10px;">
        </div>
        <div style="flex: 1; background: #E4E2F8; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; font-size: 14pt; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
            <h3>Lesson 2 - Intro to CSS</h3>
            <div>Styling decisions are where accessibility either happens on purpose or gets missed entirely. This lesson covers CSS and how it shapes the user experience of a web app, along with the POUR accessibility principles and how to apply them when styling a page.<br><br><em>20 minutes</em></div>
        </div>
    </div>
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 15px;">
        <div style="width: 20%; max-width: 120px; aspect-ratio: 1 / 1; background: #FFFFFF; border-radius: 20px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0;">
            <img src="https://images.code.org/76c4e5b2632895e2ff6513ffb63ee5fc-image-1785425093199.png" alt="Lesson 3 Icon" style="width: 65%; height: 65%; object-fit: contain; border-radius: 10px;">
        </div>
        <div style="flex: 1; background: #FFFFFF; border: 1px solid #FFFFFF; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; font-size: 14pt; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
            <h3>Lesson 3 - Interactivity in Web Apps</h3>
            <div>Event listeners and handlers are the mechanism behind every button click and page update your students will build. This lesson introduces JavaScript as the language that makes a web app respond to users, and how the DOM updates a page in real time.<br><br><em>25 minutes</em></div>
        </div>
    </div>
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 15px;">
        <div style="width: 20%; max-width: 120px; aspect-ratio: 1 / 1; background: #FFFFFF; border: 1px solid #ffffff; border-radius: 20px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0;">
            <img src="https://images.code.org/3f12c0d8b05f89f5ee40071c08233249-image-1785425200781.png" alt="Lesson 4 Icon" style="width: 65%; height: 65%; object-fit: contain; border-radius: 10px;">
        </div>
        <div style="flex: 1; background: #E4E2F8; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; font-size: 14pt; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
            <h3>Lesson 4 - JS Functions, Conditionals, and Loops</h3>
            <div>Reusable functions, if/else conditionals, and loops: these three building blocks show up in nearly everything students write from here on. You will see how these pieces let students build programs that make decisions and repeat actions.<br><br><em>25 minutes</em></div>
        </div>
    </div>
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 15px;">
        <div style="width: 20%; max-width: 120px; aspect-ratio: 1 / 1; background: #ffffff; border-radius: 20px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0;">
            <img src="https://images.code.org/b39dda06cfbafec7aef8b85d0c3caed1-image-1740592349350.png" alt="Lesson 5 Icon" style="width: 65%; height: 65%; object-fit: contain; border-radius: 10px;">
        </div>
        <div style="flex: 1; background: #FFFFFF; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; font-size: 14pt; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
            <h3>Lesson 5 - Preparing for the Final Project</h3>
            <div>Guiding students through a final project takes more than good code checks. This lesson focuses on key instructional strategies including collaboration methods, structured feedback, and time management planning.<br><br><em>10 minutes</em></div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

---

## Engaging with This Module
Feel free to complete as much or as little of this module as is useful for you and your students! 
Stop, start, and revisit content as you see fit. We hope you'll find these activities useful to start teaching this unit. 
You'll be able to give Code.org your feedback on the last level of this module.
MARKDOWN
